{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 names a 'combinatorial diversity' integration but defines the covering principle as mere non-intersection of dependency sets. This collapses under the problem's own distinguishing criterion: nodes can have disjoint dependency sets yet still share a hidden common-cause trigger (e.g., a leap-second bug present independently in each node's own time library). The 'single shared substrate' refuter only catches common-dependency correlation, leaving apparent-independence-via-disjoint-dependencies unrefuted, so the integration does not actually distinguish independent from merely uncorrelated failure.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 rests the contradiction on a 'diversity coefficient' but its refuter concedes the decisive point: if a monolith with N-version programming reaches the same failure-isolation probability, then diversity coefficient and total redundancy are compatible, not contradictory. By making the relation 'refuted if' the two metrics coincide, it undermines its own claim of contradiction and never establishes the covering principle that would tell independent isolation apart from merely uncorrelated implementations before a shared software bug is discovered.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_003","attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's 'Recovery Synchronization' discriminator is not a covering principle for independence vs apparent independence; it is a symptom that correlated triggers often produce, not a defining condition. It fails for whole classes the problem asks to distinguish: a shared environmental trigger (e.g., regional power loss) produces synchronized recovery for genuinely independent services simply because the external cause clears at once, while genuinely independent hardware failures can recover in a synchronized burst under uniform SLA/automation. Thus synchronized recovery neither entails correlation nor rules out independence.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_004","attack":true,"case":"SRC_004's 'intentional inefficiency / sub-optimal diversity' mechanism is itself not independent resilience: it concedes that fragmentation is the driver, but fragmentation can be purely superficial while a shared cause persists underneath (e.g., diverse protocol implementations all depending on one CA, DNS provider, or OS kernel). It therefore cannot distinguish truly independent failure modes from failures that merely appear uncorrelated until the shared substrate is revealed; it even admits a countercondition (physical-layer event ignoring software diversity) that breaks the mechanism, yet offers no replacement discriminator.","counterexample":null}]}